---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import AgentAccess from "../components/AgentAccess.astro";
import { href, repo, roles } from "../lib/catalog";
import { magicswordURL } from "../lib/magicsword-links.mjs";
---

<Layout title="About & Methodology — OpenAttacks" active="about">
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">OPEN KNOWLEDGE. BETTER DEFENSE.</span>
      <h1>Evidence before assumptions.</h1>
      <p>
        OpenAttacks is a community-maintained catalog of reported attacks and
        security research where AI is part of the attack path. A MagicSword
        project, built for defenders.
      </p>
    </header>
    <article class="prose">
      <h2 id="methodology">How to read this catalog</h2>
      <p>
        Each entry describes what the source reports, how AI was involved, the
        tools and victims that are publicly documented, and stage-specific
        defensive opportunities. Researchers and organizations receive prominent
        credit, and every entry links back to its sources.
      </p>
      <p>
        The initial catalog contains 68 entries spanning 2023–2026. It includes
        observed campaigns, research demonstrations, vulnerability reports, and
        uncertain marketplace claims. Research and borderline entries are
        labeled individually. This is a curated record of reports, rather than a
        complete measurement of attack prevalence.
      </p>
      <h2>What counts as AI involvement?</h2>
      <p>
        AI can act as an operator, a lure, a target, infrastructure, a code
        generator, or a source of credentials to steal. We classify the primary
        role and record a secondary role when the evidence supports it.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {Object.values(roles).map((role) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{role.label}.</strong> {role.description}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <h2>Confidence and uncertainty</h2>
      <p>
        Confidence describes how strongly the evidence supports AI’s
        involvement, not the severity of an attack. High means direct evidence;
        medium means credible reporting with material limits; low means indirect
        or disputed evidence. Unrated records retain a missing assessment in the
        exports. The interface shows confidence only when an assessment is
        supplied; we do not infer it from the researcher or publisher.
      </p>
      <p>
        Browse titles, concise summaries, environments and evidence classes are
        editorial annotations in the{" "}
        <a href={href("api/catalog.json")}>browse index</a>. Evidence labels
        distinguish campaigns, vulnerability reports, research, provider
        reports, analysis and advertised claims; they describe the source
        account and do not independently verify an incident. Evidence
        qualifications appear before each diagram. Original names and full
        descriptions remain in the technical record and complete exports.
      </p>
      <p>
        “Unknown” preserves an information gap. Report dates can have day or
        month precision. Month-only dates remain month-only in the interface and
        exports; sorting places them at the beginning of that month.
      </p>
      <h2>Sources and review dates</h2>
      <p>
        The seed collection was supplied as a research snapshot dated October
        20, 2026. The seed review dates come from that snapshot and do not imply
        that the site independently reverified every underlying claim.
        Contributors should update <code>last_reviewed</code> only after
        reviewing the source material.
      </p>
      <h2>What applies to your stack?</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href={href("stack/")}>stack explorer</a> matches your selected
        tools and operating systems against reviewed victim-side product and
        workflow conditions. An attacker using an AI product does not establish
        exposure for users of that product. Each match gives a reason,
        conditions to check, and a first action. Historical vulnerability
        reports still require an affected-version check. Research findings
        retain their evidence qualifications.
      </p>
      <p>
        These are editorial mappings, not an inventory scan or a risk score.
        Coverage is incomplete and missing matches do not establish safety.
        Selections stay in the page URL for sharing; matching runs in your
        browser. The <a href={href("api/relevance.json")}>relevance index</a>
        publishes the same rules, source references and assessment status for
        every record. New entries remain unassessed until their conditions are
        reviewed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Geography is normalized to reported targets. Parent organizations, actor
        language, hosting infrastructure, and submission telemetry are not
        counted as separate victim locations. Uncertain or excluded original
        labels are explained in region notes. “Multiple regions” retains
        multi-country scope without inventing specific countries. The{" "}
        <a href={href("regions/")}>region breakdown</a> shows supported target
        locations and keeps unknown or unspecified geography separate.
      </p>
      <h2 id="for-agents">Read with an AI assistant</h2>
      <p>
        Start with <a href={href("llms.txt")}>llms.txt</a> for an entry index
        and query guide, or load the{" "}
        <a href={href("llms-full.txt")}>complete Markdown context</a>.
        Individual records are available as JSON and Markdown under{" "}
        <code>api/attacks/&#123;id&#125;.json</code> and{" "}
        <code>api/attacks/&#123;id&#125;.md</code>. The{" "}
        <a href={href("api/schema.json")}>public schema</a> describes every
        field.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site and all AI-readable files are generated from the same YAML
        collection on every merged attack PR through the deployment workflow.
        Counts and entry links update automatically. The API is static:
        assistants download the JSON array and apply filters locally.
      </p>
      <h2>Use and contribute</h2>
      <p>
        The public <a href={href("api/attacks.json")}>JSON</a> and{" "}
        <a href={href("api/attacks.csv")}>CSV</a> exports are generated from the
        same validated YAML files as the site. Internal publishing schedules and
        draft URLs are excluded. All claims remain attributable to their
        original sources.
      </p>
      <p>
        Add an entry or suggest a correction through{" "}
        <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`}>the contribution guide</a>
        . Every claim needs a source URL; uncertain details should say
        “unknown.” Do not include live victim data or unredacted victim IP
        addresses.
      </p>
      <h2>From knowledge to prevention</h2>
      <p>
        The prevention notes connect reported behavior to practical defensive
        controls. Defensive notes remain in the JSON and Markdown exports. Entry
        diagrams show source-linked activity and preserve unknowns. ATLAS
        annotations map described behavior to MITRE’s AI-specific technique
        catalog; they are independent catalog annotations.
      </p>
      <p>
        Explore{" "}
        <a href={magicswordURL("https://magicsword.io/", "about")}>
          free MagicSword.io
        </a>{" "}
        for community resources to support your defensive work.
      </p>
    </article>
    <AgentAccess />
  </div>
</Layout>
